## 8. idr-expect properties

**`Lookup.cpp`:**
- remove `in-bounds`, `bounds-checked`, `pure-array-loops`;
- add `structured-loops`, `typed-reads`, `in-place`, `fused`;
- keep `no-guards`, `vectorized`, `narrowed-lanes`, `moves-out` (rewritten).

`Properties.h` declarations follow. `Expect.cpp`'s `dependentDialects` in Passes.td add `tensor::TensorDialect`, `scf::SCFDialect`, `linalg::LinalgDialect`, because quantities-kept parses an emitted module that holds tensor and scf ops. All four new properties take `@f` the way `rootsOf`/`named` do (`Properties.h:41-58`). The new ones live in `Expect/Arrays.cpp` and `Expect/Guards.cpp`.

- **`structured-loops=@f`.** Fails when:
  - @f holds no `linalg::LinalgOp`;
  - an `scf.for` in @f is a *pure array write*:
    - tensor form: a perfect nest whose innermost yield is `tensor.insert` into its iter_arg at exactly the nest's IVs;
    - memref form: a `memref.store` into a memref defined above the nest at exactly its IVs;
    - with every other op in the nest satisfying `idr::onlyAllocates`;
  - an `scf.for` is a *reassociable fold*: one scalar integer iter_arg combined by a D15 case-1 op as its only use, the rest only computing;
  - some linalg payload in @f holds an op that does not only compute (ignoring `ReferenceResource` effects of `idr.dup`/`idr.drop` after rc).

  Ordered folds and scans are not array writes or case-1 folds and are allowed. Error format: `expected structured-loops: a pure loop over an array stayed an scf.for in @g`.
- **`typed-reads=@f`.** @f holds at least one typed access: `tensor.extract`/`insert`/`extract_slice`/`insert_slice`, `memref.load`/`store`, `vector.transfer_read`/`write`/`load`/`store`/`gather`, or a linalg op. None of the index operands of those ops, looked through `arith.index_cast`/`index_castui`, is the result of an op implementing `GuardOpInterface`. World-ordered accesses keep their guard (§13) and are not typed accesses.
- **`in-place=@f`.** Checked on modules after idr-bufferize. @f holds a write to an array (`memref.store`, or a linalg op with a memref init), and no copy into a fresh buffer: no `linalg.copy`, identity-copy `linalg.generic` (one input, one init, identity maps, payload `linalg.yield %in`) or `memref.copy` whose destination is a `memref.alloc` result, directly or through `memref.cast`. Copies into a `memref.subview` are `insert_slice`'s meaning (read: `M/lib/Dialect/Tensor/Transforms/BufferizableOpInterfaceImpl.cpp:730`), not conflict copies.
- **`fused=@f`.** @f holds a linalg op, and:
  - tensor form: no linalg result with exactly one use where that use is an input of another linalg op in @f;
  - memref form: no `memref.alloc` whose uses are exactly the init of one linalg op and inputs of one other linalg op.

  Duplication of a producer with two consumers is excluded by upstream's default control (one use), not checked.
- **`moves-out[=@f]`** (rewritten for memrefs, after idr-rc). Some counted `memref.load` whose result is consumed with no `idr.dup` of it or of a view of it. The next `memref.store` to the same memref SSA value at the same index SSA values follows in its block, and no `idr.drop` of a `memref.load` of that slot lies between them.
- **`vectorized`:** description "after the vectorization schedule (`transform-interpreter`)". Logic unchanged (`Vectorized.cpp`).

